7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51adb7d7-3c91-52a4-abdc-6e654000342b" :question/math-academy-id "q-87088" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{8}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/84a3340f9154465fb82fff3839a99303ead9f43462c6091ff2856e946f69746f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "713fc33d-83da-590f-9f68-03deb34d7745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308835} {:answer/id #uuid "896cd95c-a469-50db-a939-47330ea46144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308836} {:answer/id #uuid "44143780-c8cd-55cd-9fe2-f289fdbbbfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308837} {:answer/id #uuid "c69d2fdb-8d95-5e19-9150-433c74826146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308838} {:answer/id #uuid "5a10463e-18ff-55ca-a408-e678a6c87bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a10463e-18ff-55ca-a408-e678a6c87bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8deeb3c-b80e-5f4d-baab-4e4a74ff10dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49164e78-91b8-549a-8993-e1fab20375b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87125" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/c010f583fc33ee3c5335c16c0aaf1c20c2e5cf82d49fe7cf14e8e5661bf202fc.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c04524-f586-5840-aca7-1e94e4e2f476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280889} {:answer/id #uuid "224bcd89-5caa-5691-ba4b-2f4d6fcc70fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280890} {:answer/id #uuid "167202a1-76a6-5739-962c-6f02e75b82c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280891} {:answer/id #uuid "1a41196c-4d3b-58e9-a563-55f2e4cedc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280892} {:answer/id #uuid "b069363a-92f8-593b-9e3b-c08620bad3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "167202a1-76a6-5739-962c-6f02e75b82c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280891} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2decc71-3062-55f2-a60e-3571d178a7b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9cc0f30-0e30-5e6d-ac7f-35bd27e7673c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87139" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/386a4b395d45276284606b0f079be16881af717ba7e791ab62d8df34743f0269.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$"}]}
